  <title>Another computer</title>
  <p>The bricks can run on one computer, the lab machine, and the windows on
  another, your desktop. The lab machine runs Virtualbricks with
  <cmd>--listen</cmd>, with its own windows or without, and your desktop
  opens its windows with <cmd>--connect</cmd>. Both run the same version of
  Virtualbricks.</p>
  <screen># on the lab machine
virtualbricks --no-gui --no-term --listen \
    'ssl:8765:interface=0.0.0.0:privateKey=~/vb/lab.key'
# on your desktop
virtualbricks --connect 'ssl:lab.example:8765:caCertsDir=~/vb/lab'</screen>
  <p>A connection over the network proves that it knows the token of the lab
  machine, the file <file>~/.config/virtualbricks/token</file>, so copy it to
  your desktop; or the lab machine asks for the certificate of your desktop
  instead. <cmd>virtualbricks --connect</cmd> alone opens the windows of
  a Virtualbricks of yours on this computer, started with
  <cmd>--listen</cmd>.</p>
  <p if:test="!target:html">How to listen and connect, the token and the
  certificates are in <link href="man:virtualbricks(1)">virtualbricks(1)</link>,
  and the protocols in
  <link href="man:virtualbricks-control(7)">virtualbricks-control(7)</link>.</p>
  <p if:test="target:html">How to listen and connect, the token and the
  certificates are in
  <link href="https://www.virtualbricks.it/documentation/command-line.html">virtualbricks(1)</link>,
  and the protocols in
  <link href="https://www.virtualbricks.it/documentation/control-protocols.html">virtualbricks-control(7)</link>.</p>
  <section id="windows">
    <title>What the windows do</title>
    <list>
      <item><p>They follow the project open there, whoever changes it, and
      what you do goes there: a start, a new brick, the <gui>OK</gui> of the
      settings of a brick.</p></item>
      <item><p>What they show is the lab machine's: its QEMU programs, its
      USB devices, its files and its projects. A path is a path there, typed
      with the completion of its folders.</p></item>
      <item><p>The consoles of the bricks open in a terminal of your
      desktop.</p></item>
      <item><p><gui>Settings</gui> has a page for your desktop, one for the
      lab machine, and one for the project.</p></item>
      <item><p>Import, export, the long work on images and <gui>Show in
      Files</gui> wait for a later version, greyed.</p></item>
    </list>
    <p>When the connection is lost, a bar says why, and
    <gui>Reconnect</gui> connects again. <gui>Quit</gui> closes the windows:
    the Virtualbricks on the lab machine goes on.</p>
  </section>
